#!/usr/bin/python3
"""AresOS desktop supervisor: boots the session into the AresHub world (package 23).

Design: docs/design/OS-BASE.md section 5 and Appendices B1, B2 and B5.

labwc's autostart runs `aresos-desktop session`, a small keeper that runs the
supervisor (`aresos-desktop supervise`) and restarts it if it fails. The
supervisor
  - checks the image's pinned Minecraft inputs (/usr/share/aresos/minecraft),
  - creates the managed Prism instance AresDesktop and the worlds folder the
    first time, and never changes an existing instance or world,
  - starts the app helper (/usr/libexec/aresos-shell) and Prism
    (`prismlauncher --launch AresDesktop --world AresHub`) in its own systemd
    user scope,
  - learns about the real Java process from the instance's wrapper command
    (/usr/libexec/aresos-desktop-java), which asks it for the launch's
    environment, reports the JVM's PID and later its exit status,
  - learns readiness from the helper's status pipe (world_ready/heartbeat),
  - and applies B2's restart policy.

The startup cover (package 23b, /usr/libexec/aresos-cover) hides Minecraft's own
start (Prism's launch dialog, Mojang's loading screen, joining the world) for
the session's first hub launch and the owner's retry, never for an automatic
restart, setup, or the retry after AUTH_REQUIRED (Prism's sign-in must be
seen). A crash under the cover keeps it through the automatic restart, and the
restarted hub takes the focus. It ends when the hub is ready, when a message
window has to be seen, when Prism has not handed the game over 25 s after its
start (it may be waiting for him in a dialog of its own), when he presses
Super+Ctrl+Escape for the recovery terminal (`aresos-desktop hide-cover`), and
when the supervisor stops. It is best effort: a cover that does not start,
crashes or is missing changes nothing else.

States (B2): SETUP (first start: Prism's own sign-in, then the hub is created
with the mod's AresOS button), STARTING, IN_WORLD, AUTH_REQUIRED and RECOVERY.
SETUP, AUTH_REQUIRED and RECOVERY show a foot terminal with what happened and
what to do. Restart policy: an unexpected game exit is retried after a short
backoff; three failures within five minutes or three in a row latch RECOVERY.
The latch and the counts live in ~/.local/state/aresos/desktop.json and survive
restarts of the supervisor and of the machine. Only 30 continuous healthy
minutes in the world (valid heartbeats) or the owner's `retry` reset them.
Auth states, deliberate quits and kills (memory pressure) never restart by
themselves, and nothing here ever kills a running game or repairs a world.

Only a certain ordinary crash restarts by itself (JUDGMENT-CALL 10): Java's own
exit status, as its wrapper saw it, other than 0 (his quit) and SIGKILL. An
end by SIGKILL, whoever sent it, and an end nobody explains latch RECOVERY and
wait for the owner's `retry`.

Socket: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/aresos/desktop.sock, one JSON object per line, same
user only. The wrapper sends jvm_start, jvm_started, and how Java ended
(jvm_exited, or jvm_failed for a start that did not become a game); it writes
the same facts to ~/.local/state/aresos/desktop-exit.json first. The owner's
commands (`aresos-desktop status|retry|forget-hub|reset-instance`) use status,
retry and forget_hub.

The lifecycle channel (package 25; OS-BASE B1, `save_world`): the game gets
ARESOS_DESKTOP_LIFECYCLE=1, and its mod then connects to the same socket with
`lifecycle_hello`. The hello is accepted only from the JVM the wrapper
reported, with this session's instance and the launch's nonce. After it the
supervisor sends `save_world` and reads `world_saved`. A save counts only
when the game says the active world (the home the helper last reported
ready, else the locked world) reached the disk, with the request's id and on
the connection the request went out on. `aresos-desktop save` (socket op
`save`) asks for one and exits 0 only then. No reply blocks the loop: a peer
that does not take its line at once is dropped.

One stop routine (package 25, brief section 2): `aresos-desktop stop` (socket
op `stop`) ends the game on purpose, inside one deadline of 60 s. A stop record
goes into desktop.json first; then the active world is saved and the answer
checked; then Java gets SIGTERM through its pidfd, and the supervisor waits
for its real exit and for the desktop lock. A launch that ends while a stop
record names it is `stopped`: never counted, restarted or latched `killed`.
A stop is confirmed (saved, or nothing to save) or unsaved. A plain stop whose
save is not confirmed is called off and the game keeps running; `stop --reason
shutdown` cannot be called off, closes anyway and latches `unsaved` for the
next start. The supervisor never sends SIGKILL to the game.

The black screen and the lid (package 25, brief 3.4; the owner on board #379:
no visible shutdown wait, and a lid closed right after must not suspend the
laptop mid-save). A shutdown, logout or restart goes black at its start
(`aresos-cover --black`, one window on every output) when the helper's last
`windows` report says that no app window is open; with an app window open, or
no report, the screens stay as they are. It ends when the stop is called off,
fails or overruns (a window must be seen), when an app window appears, when a
restart's startup cover has taken over, when labwc ends, or by its own limit;
at a shutdown it stays until the session is torn down. For the length of every
stop a `systemd-inhibit` child holds a logind block on the lid switch and on
sleep; it is started and never waited for.
"""
